<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<!--
    Fragment: which parts of its colour variant a block actually paints.

    A variant describes two surfaces that reach every block: the block itself,
    and the content wrapper inside it. Each of them has a background and a
    border, and each of those four is an independent answer. An outlined block
    with no fill is a common ask, and so is a filled block with no outline: the
    variant proposes, the block disposes.

    Everything is on by default, so a block that never opens this group keeps
    the whole variant, which is what published content already does.

    The two surfaces a variant can also paint, the paragraph and the accent,
    are deliberately absent. The paragraph exists on five blocks out of sixteen
    and the accent on two, so a checkbox for them would do nothing on every
    other block, and a dead checkbox teaches editors that our settings do not
    work. They get their own fragment the day a block needs them.

    It closes the `settings` section, after the radius fields:

        <xi:include href="../fragments/block-surfaces.xml"
                    xpointer="xmlns(sulu=http://schemas.sulu.io/template/template) xpointer(/sulu:properties/sulu:property)"/>

    Every block includes it, `separator` excepted: that one paints a surface
    like the others but declares its own background toggler, since a rule or a
    gap is something an editor fills and unfills often.
    `BlockSurfaceToggleContractTest` keeps that exception list honest.
-->
<properties xmlns="http://schemas.sulu.io/template/template"
            xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <!--
        A heading, and a deliberate one: it names the group, and it spans the
        full width, which resets the two-column grid. Without that reset an odd
        number of half-width fields above pushes every pair below out of step,
        and a margin selector full of boxes ends up beside a plain select. The
        count above cannot be fixed by hand either, since visibility conditions
        change it at runtime.

        It also moves these fields out from under the radius heading, where the
        lone background checkbox used to read as a corner setting.
    -->
    <property name="surfacesHeading" type="heading">
        <params>
            <param name="label">
                <meta><title>iw_sulu_tailwind_theme.settings_group_surfaces</title></meta>
            </param>
        </params>
    </property>

    <!--
        The block background keeps its own fragment and its original property
        name. Both are public: a project composing its own blocks may include
        the fragment directly, and every page already published carries the
        `showBackground` key.
    -->
    <xi:include href="block-background.xml"
                xpointer="xmlns(sulu=http://schemas.sulu.io/template/template) xpointer(/sulu:properties/sulu:property)"/>

    <property name="showBlockBorder" type="checkbox" colspan="6">
        <meta><title>iw_sulu_tailwind_theme.show_block_border</title></meta>
        <params>
            <param name="default_value" value="true"/>
        </params>
    </property>

    <property name="showContentBackground" type="checkbox" colspan="6">
        <meta><title>iw_sulu_tailwind_theme.show_content_background</title></meta>
        <params>
            <param name="default_value" value="true"/>
        </params>
    </property>

    <!--
        Turning both content fields off removes the padding of that surface as
        well, since it has none of its own: the block pads itself through its
        own fields, the content surface only through a theme token. So the only
        question it can answer is whether it exists at all.
    -->
    <property name="showContentBorder" type="checkbox" colspan="6">
        <meta><title>iw_sulu_tailwind_theme.show_content_border</title></meta>
        <params>
            <param name="default_value" value="true"/>
        </params>
    </property>
</properties>
